\startsection[title={Workflow}]
% story: new challenges by sc data; old way to deal with data (workflows) are not efficient → new approaches needed → me

% Overview:Describe how the workflows work and why the aforementioned is important.
The general workflow of scRNA-Seq data processing consists of five steps.
The first step, library preparation, is experimental and takes place in a biological laboratory.
The second step, quantification, creates a cell × gene matrix from raw data produced in step one.
The remaining steps are preprocessing, analysis and visualization of this matrix.

\startsubsection[title={Library Preparation and Sequencing}]
% 1. (cells → transcript library) and sequencing (transcript library → reads)
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:rna-seq,title={Library preparation, sequencing, and mapping}]
% https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Summary_of_RNA-Seq.svg
\externalfigure[Summary_of_RNA-Seq.svg][width=.7\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

It begins with an experiment to prepare and sequence a transcript library containing mostly mature mRNA but also unspliced mRNA and other RNA (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]a).
There are different kinds of library preparation protocols, all of which have in common that they isolate and fragment the RNA per single cell, then tag each fragment with a cell specific barcode for later assignment to their origin cell.
The most important differences between protocols manifest in cost, sensitivity and the distinction between those capturing paired fragments starting at both ends of the RNA and those capturing only the RNA end where translation into proteins starts (the 3′ end) \cite[ziegenhain2018].
The sequencing machine finally digitizes the fragments and allows all further processing to happen on a computer (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]b).
\TODO[give figure an a, b, c]
% 1½. protocol specific bias correction
Additional to the cell barcode, some protocols allow to enrich the library with information that can be used to reduce sources of noise or bias.
This includes e.\,g. unique molecular identifiers (UMIs), which allow to count each transcript only once despite a common protocol step involving multiple incomplete duplications of all transcripts \cite[islam2014].
A second example is adding known quantities of RNA from a foreign organism (spike-in RNA) to each cell library, which later allows to correct for nonlinear per-cell biases in transcript counts \cite[lun2017b].
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Counting}]
% 2. (reads → counts)
\TODO[or quantification?]
Counting starts from the paired sequences read from both ends of a fragment (paired-end reads) or the 3′ end sequences (single-end reads).
The read or read pair includes a cell barcode and possibly a UMI barcode.
\footnote{\inlinecite[uzbas2019] developed BART-Seq, a protocol that combines paired-end reads with two cell barcodes to address a high number of cells.}
Classic counting uses specialized short read alignment tools like \n{Bowtie} \cite[langmead2009,langmead2012] or \n{STAR} \cite[dobin2013] to match reads to a reference genome annotated with information about where genes start, end, and can be spliced.
Aligned reads are counted per splicing configuration that translates to a protein, while collapsing the counts for reads with identical UMIs – if available (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]c).
Since only the resulting count matrix is needed for many kinds of downstream analysis, there have been advances in pseudo-alignment algorithms like \n{kallisto} \cite[ntranos2016] or \n{Salmon} \cite[patro2017], which efficiently count without a separate alignment step.
For ease of use, pipelines wrapping those tools for certain experimental protocols exist.
Those include \n{Cell Ranger} for \n{10x Genomics}’ \n{Chromium} protocol \cite[zheng2017] or \n{zUMIs} \cite[parekh2018] for several protocols involving UMIs.
\TODO[mention my bartseq pipeline here?]
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[reference=sec:preprocessing, title={Preprocessing}]
% 3. (counts → expressions)
% generally \cite[luecken2018]: https://www.embopress.org/doi/10.15252/msb.20188746
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:batch-effect,location=right,title={
Batch effect correction reduces biases between data gathered in multiple sequencer runs.
Cells from well-normalized data will have similar expression profiles to cells from the other sequencer runs.
\small{(Figure reproduced with friendly permission from \inlinecite[buttner2019])}
}]
\externalfigure[batch-effect-correction.svg][width=.5\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

In order to obtain comparable quantities, raw counts have to be preprocessed to correct for various technical biases.
Commonly one starts by eliminating rows and columns from the count matrix that are unlikely to be (living) cells or important genes.
This filtering removes rows and columns containing a low count sum or very few entries.
Rows like this are assumed to be empty droplets or wells, while columns like this are genes that are not informative of the observed cell variation.
It also removes cells containing a high fraction of mitochondrial counts, indicative of dead or dying cells \cite[ilicic2016].
Doublets cannot be detected reliably by a simple count threshold, as a pair of small cells can have similar numbers of expressed genes and counts as single large cell, but more sophisticated solutions are emerging \cite[mcginnis2019,wolock2019].
\inlinecite[luecken2019] point out that each of those filtering covariates in isolation is not sufficient to identify a non-cell row, and suggests conservative joint scoring using more than one covariate.

Another common preprocessing step is normalization.
It entails correction for common technical sources of cell variation, as well as biological sources with a strong effect, such as the cell cycle:
when not studying the cell cycle itself, its variation can obscure the processes under investigation \cite[buettner2015].
If multiple runs of the sequencer were used to obtain the full data, there will be biases per run that can be reduced by using a dedicated batch effect correction algorithm (see \infig[fig:batch-effect]).
While older bulk correction effects such as \n{ComBat} \cite[johnson2007] have proven to be successful in the last years, a growing number of dedicated scRNA-Seq batch effect correction tools is becoming available \cite[haghverdi2018,lotfollahi2018,korsunsky2019,polanski2020].
Preprocessing can also involve scaling count values using a per-cell function derived from the aforementioned spike-ins.
All the aforementioned transformation have to be carefully balanced with their potential to over-correct and remove relevant biological variation.

A final common count matrix preprocessing step is to reduce data by discarding genes with low variability, as they contribute little to the data’s variability.
This highly variable gene selection is performed with differently rigorous algorithms \cite[brennecke2013,yip2018].
This more complex filtering is performed in addition to the aforementioned quality-based gene filtering, reducing the retained number of genes to the low thousands \cite[luecken2019].

One step occupies the area between preprocessing and analysis.
In order to model or otherwise analyze count data, it is almost always necessary to calculate distances between cells’ expression profiles.
Distance measures usually expect data with no mean–variance dependency (i.\,e. normally distributed data), which can be fulfilled in two ways.
One is to transform expressions into a space where those expectations hold, commonly by logarithmizing it.
The intuition is that in in log-space, distances represent log-fold changes, an usual way to compare expression levels \cite[brennecke2013,eling2018].
Another way is to employ dimensional reduction methods to embed the data into a lower-dimensional space with the desired properties, such as Principal Component Analysis (PCA, \inlinecite[abdi2010]) or diffusion maps \cite[coifman2005].
An advantage of this method are the reduced computational and memory costs of operating on a low-dimensional embedding.
\inlinecite[svensson2020] suggested that log-transforming data is a possible cause for distorting data by zero-inflation.
With single cell data specific transforms like \n{SCTransform} \cite[hafemeister2019], as well as embeddings like \n{scVI} \cite[lopez2018] or \n{GLM-PCA} \cite[townes2019], promising alternatives are emerging, despite them losing the log-fold interpretability.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Analysis}]
% 4. uncover dynamics, heterogeneity structure, variable importance and role
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:waddington,location=left,title={
Cells differentiate smoothly, in stochasically diverging paths, and not always in the same general direction.
\TODO[better use linear discrimination using marker genes? or smoothly diverging developmental markers?]
}]
%https://anatomypubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/dvdy.24678
\externalfigure[https://anatomypubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/cms/asset/aafdf349-9324-496d-822d-183edae5d947/dvdy24678-fig-0001-m.jpg][width=.5\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

With unwanted sources of noise and variability removed or reduced, the data can be analyzed.
Analysis of single cell data generally focuses on uncovering previously unknown heterogeneity and its sources \cite[patel2014,poirion2016,angerer2017].
The kind of heterogeneity to uncover depends on the kind of data available:
the simplest case is discrimination between two discrete subpopulations from different experimental conditions.
A common goal there is to find a small number of marker genes able to reliably distinguish a subpopulation that is sufficiently distinct from the rest of the cells.
Methods performing this search are called differential gene expression (DE) analyses \cite[soneson2018].
While DE analysis methods stem from bulk RNA-Seq, single-cell specific methods such as SCDE \cite[kharchenko2014], MAST \cite[finak2015], and BASiCS \cite[vallejos2016] outperform bulk methods due to the additional information in single cell data.

Another source of heterogeneity is the composition of the studied tissue.
Tumors as well as many kinds of healthy tissue are composed of cells fulfilling a multitude of different roles that can only be uncovered using single cell analysis \cite[patel2014,zhang2016,qian2017].
A common method to make sense of complex heterogeneous data is cluster analysis.
For single cell data, the multi-resolution community detection algorithm \n{Leiden} \cite[traag2019] and its predecessor \n{Louvain} is used most commonly, despite \inlinecite[kiselev2019] arguing its lack of performance for smaller datasets.
A general problem of clustering is data reuse.
Clusters are commonly the basis for further analysis like inter-cluster DE analysis – a task the aforementioned single cell specific DE tools are specifically designed for.
However, this leads to both clusters and markers being derived from the same data, and treating clusters as ground truth in this settings leads to inflated confidence in the found markers.
The problem can be circumvented by joint clustering and DE analysis, manually propagating uncertainties \cite[zhang2019], or not relying on clustering to detect genes \cite[angerer2020].

While cluster analysis is greatly enriched by scRNA-Seq analysis, the field of trajectory analysis can only exist due to this kind of data.
Traditionally, cells were thought of exhibiting a discrete cell type, which still applies to cells that are dramatically specialized for their roles.
Most tissues however contain less discrete cell types, like the pluripotent stem cells from which all cells derive \cite[haghverdi2015].
While marker genes for certain cell roles or developmental steps exist, this complex and gradual nature of most tissues requires sophisticated analysis and visualization of single cell data.
Early trajectory analysis tools like \n{Wanderlust} \cite[bendall2014], or DPT \cite[haghverdi2016] can effectively applied to simple developmental data.
But while cells usually get increasingly specialized during their path of development, the reverse direction (dedifferentiation) can also be observed (see \infig[fig:waddington]).
Furthermore, developing cells do not necessarily follow all steps of differentiation in the same order or magnitude.
This results in cells temporarily or sometimes permanently stuck as intermediate types.
The field of trajectory analysis is therefore constantly evolving to enable the analysis of complex tissues up to whole animals \cite[plass2018] using tools like SPRING \cite[weinreb2018a] or PAGA \cite[wolf2019].

Further analyses can also depend on the availability of other kinds of data \cite[efremova2020].
As an example, RNA velocity is an indication of the direction in which cells differentiate and therefore enhances trajectory inference with directionality and more accuracy.
It can be reconstructed from the relation of spliced and unspliced mRNA \cite[lamanno2018].
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Visualization}]
% 5. (results often too complex to understand as numbers)
% talk about dim red here and the fact that it’s hard to reason about how the input variables relate back to output – make path for GR coming to the rescue
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:embeddings, title={
Four increasingly complex two dimensional embeddings of the same cells.
The differentiating cells are colored by age in days.
\bold{(a)} Two genes, even carefully selected markers, are not able to separate the differentiation paths \bold{(b)} A PCA is able to differentiate clusters, but not like \bold{(c)} diffusion maps, bifurcations and smooth transitions.
\bold{(d)} UMAPs use machine learning, allowing here to flatten multiple kinds of differences into a 2D plot, as in this case both bifurcation events present in the data.
}]
\externalfigure[dim-red.pdf][width=\textwidth]
%\input{imgs/dim-red.tikz}
\stopplacefigure

The complexity of the heterogeneity being uncovered not only reflects in analysis methods but also the visualizations of their results.
Complex developmental hierarchies and other relations in the data need to be faithfully represented.
This commonly involves laying out points representing the cells in a flat plane while trying to maintain relationships such as developmental closeness (see \infig[fig:embeddings]).
This general process also employs more complex dimensional reduction methods than the ones used for distance calculations (see \insec[sec:preprocessing]).
And the more complex a process is, the more likely it is that this process contains steps that cannot be traced back.

Without additional data providing a ground truth about subpopulation identity, or clearly separated clusters that are inherently assigned genes driving their embedding, the process of validating an embedding is manual.
A panel of marker genes per cluster is derived from the data and cross-referenced with literature to annotate known cell types in the data.
When discovering novel subpopulations, their adjacency to known ones can be used to make assumptions about their role and test them.
An upcoming way to simplify the process are cell transcriptome atlases \cite[regev2017,schaum2018] that can be used as references to automatically look up your cells and annotate them \cite[abdelaal2019].
