\stopsubsection

The above sequence of steps should be a generic overview of scRNA-Seq analysis able to serve as a framework in which to place my specific contributions.
While I worked on every computational step of the process except for alignment, my contributions are focused on steps involving a count matrix.
To provide further context for my work, I would like to outline the relevant changes in the process of scRNA-Seq analysis.
\TODO[I like this framing. does this *have* to go to “own contribs”?]

\startsubsection[title={The Old Way}]
% Generally: People often need to cobble together their own pipelines and use default parameters without knowing what they do.
% scripts and binaries
Without publicly available analysis frameworks, the most common way to operate was writing R scripts that invoked R tools in memory and tools from other languages as command line processes.
Despite the field’s increasing investment into machine learning algorithms implemented in Python, few tools were available in the language.

% communication
Communication with other languages or between pipeline steps involved reading a textual representation of the data from a file system, processing it, and writing it back to disk.
This process involved long run times due to slow reading and writing, high storage space requirements and little resilience to errors.
File corruption introduced e.\,g. via unreliable access to network storage could not be detected in text files, which lack checksums and other resilience precautions.
All those problems can be circumvented when hardening a file-based pipeline by investing sufficient effort and time.
In practice, the lack of frameworks meant that each tool or pipeline had to solve common problems by itself, which further wasted resources and increased potential for errors.
The resulting pipeline code published with papers was very application-specific and hard to generalize or share.

% Existing methods are not focused on interoperability.
Even within analysis pipelines operating entirely in memory, individual tools were not necessarily designed to be compatible.
Single tools could halt progress if they had insufficient performance for upcoming large datasets from microfluidic protocols.
The tools’ varying code quality, choice of default values and error reporting led to subtle incompatibility bugs.
Furthermore, no data structures and file exchange formats suitable for scRNA-Seq existed, so workflows manually kept count matrices and annotation data frames in sync across filtering steps, increasing the potential for error.
Furthermore, reproducibility was hard to guarantee in the presence of nondeterministic methods and a multitude of tool versions with subtly different defaults and behavior.

% DM creation in R
In the R landscape, the \n{Bioconductor} project \cite[huber2015] provided tools for preprocessing and basic analysis, but little in the way of embeddings.
The only available option for diffusion map embedding at the time did not offer features necessary for scRNA-Seq analysis and was impractically slow \cite[angerer2016].
% - pre-GR: differential expression and \TODO?[trendsceek]
No dimensionality reduction method except for the simplistic PCA provides a way to relate embedding location back to input variables.
The existing and popular differential expression analysis requires discrete partitioning of the data, which is not well defined for data containing gradual transition of gene expression profiles between cells, and has the aforementioned issues with uncertainty propagation.
\TODO[mention the destiny-related stuff here? If not: just in “own contributions” and methods?]
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={The New Way}]
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:anndata, title={AnnData structure}]
\hbox{\input{imgs/anndata.tikz}}
\stopplacefigure

% - Through frameworks, data interchange formats and containers we get interoperability and reproducibility.
Today, both R and Python have viable frameworks for scRNA-Seq analysis: \n{Seurat}\footnote{
Note that \n{Seurat} in its original publication 2015 was no general purpose scRNA-Seq analysis framework yet, but a data alignment algorithm with additional helper routines.
} \cite[satija2015,butler2018], \n{scater} \cite[mccarthy2017], and \n{scanpy} \cite[wolf2018].
Their data structures allow keeping gene and cell annotations and graphs in sync with the expression matrix while filtering and concatenating data.
Conversion between the R and Python world is possible via common serialization formats and in-memory conversion \cite[angerer2019b].
%reproducibility
Reproducibility seems to be largely solved, as widely available container solutions such as \n{Docker} allow defining perfectly reproducible environments \cite[ditommaso2015].
Unlike standalone tools, the frameworks also consistently support random seeding for identical output of nondeterministic processes.
% - good error reporting / software quality important for good science (Avoid garbage-in garbage-out)
% - principle of many eyes: less bugs in shared code
The number of users of a framework or tool contributes to its quality and therefore the quality of studies relying on it, as more people report errors and special use cases, which increases the robustness of the frameworks towards those problems \cite[kim2015].
Bigger frameworks encompassing many tools have an easier time to test several permutations of their functionality than each tool would be able to when standing alone.
\TODO[last 2 sentences are almost the inverse of the “old way”: just keep it in one of the sections?]

%R
In R, the landscape is more fragmented than in Python:
The \n{Bioconductor} ecosystem provides annotated matrix data structures and tools for mainly preprocessing scRNA-Seq data.
\n{Scater} provides extensive quality control and preprocessing but little in the way of analysis \cite[luecken2019].
\n{Seurat} has opinionated preprocessing, analysis and plotting routines but is not integrated into the \n{Bioconductor} ecosystem.
Its data structure is \n{Seurat}-specific and needs conversion to the more generic \n{Bioconductor} data structures.
Standalone tools in R usually support \n{Bioconductor} data structures or even just plain matrices and data frames.

%AnnData
In Python, many standalone tools start supporting the generic data structure \n{AnnData} \cite[zappia2018,eraslan2019,polanski2020].
\n{AnnData} supports reading and writing from and to several language agnostic interchange formats.
It was the first annotated matrix data structure to support sparse expression matrices and therefore up to millions of cells.
It provides its own format that supports disk backing for operations on data too large to be performed in memory, sparse or dense.
% Scanpy
\n{AnnData} was developed alongside \n{scanpy} in order to make the latter a highly modular framework.
\n{Scanpy} is now in widespread international use, continuously expanded with new algorithms, and on its way to become a community governed project \cite[wolf2018,zappia2018].
Being based on \n{NumPy} \cite[vanderwalt2011], \n{SciPy} \cite[virtanen2020], and other mature and highly performant Python technologies, \n{scanpy} is very scalable.
% - serving all MOs: iterative with biologists, manual coding, let it be done as-a-service
Integrating well with \n{JupyterLab} and \n{Jupyter} notebooks \cite[kluyver2016], \n{scanpy} lends itself to be used interactively to quickly iterate on analyses with close feedback from biologists.
It also supports being used as a Python library to back visualization tools, web servers and traditional file-based processing pipelines.

